      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Normative current perception thresholds (CPTs) are used for the evaluation of sensory function in a variety of diseases."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "AIMS",
            "content": "To evaluate the reproducibility of CPT measurements with sinusoidal current in healthy volunteers."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Neuroselective CPT evaluations of the median and pudendal nerve in healthy volunteers were repeated with 1 week interval (T1 and T2)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "In the study group (N = 41) no difference between genders for age (MW-U: P = 0.91) and BMI (t-test: P = 0.18) were found. No significant difference between T1 and T2 was found (Paired t-test: all P-values > 0.05), although the intraclass correlation for each person was low. The variability of measures for the pudendal nerve was: ICC 2 kHz: 0.41; 250 Hz: 0.30; 5 Hz: 0.38, and for the median nerve respectively: 0.58; 0.46; 0.40. Normal CPTs were shown for the pudendal nerve: 2 kHz: 51%; 250 Hz: 76%; 5 Hz: 71%, and median nerve respectively: 78%; 98%; 80%. The pudendal nerve showed more deviating values compared to the median nerve."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Both nerves showed deviating values. CPT values with sinusoidal current assessed with 1 week interval, showed a weak intraclass correlation. This finding limits the use of CPT values with this current for longitudinal studies."
          }
        ]
      },
